#
# A small IRC server to try the IRC client against (devplan/irc.md §3a, §9), on the PC:
#
#   tools/irc-test-server --out DIR [--name HOST] [--account NAME:PASSWORD]... [--chatter SECONDS]
#
# DIR gets ca.pem, the CA its certificate is signed by: give it to the client (networks.ini's
# ca = ...). HOST is the name or address the client connects to (the certificate's). The keys
# live in a temporary folder under ~/.cache, removed when this ends.
#
#   6697  TLS        6667  plain TCP
#
# What it speaks: CAP LS 302 / REQ / END (sasl=PLAIN, server-time, message-tags, echo-message,
# multi-prefix, away-notify, extended-join, account-notify, cap-notify, chghost,
# userhost-in-names), SASL PLAIN against the --account list, NICK (433 when taken), USER,
# PING/PONG, JOIN, PART, KICK, INVITE, PRIVMSG, NOTICE, TOPIC (333: who, when), NAMES, MODE (+o +v;
# the channel's i m n p s t, k key, l limit; the b, e and I lists: 367/368, 348/349, 346/347),
# AWAY, WHOIS, LIST (with ELIST=U: >n, <n; --channels made-up channels besides the real ones),
# MONITOR (+ - C L S; 730/731 as people come and go; --no-monitor: without, for ISON), ISON,
# QUIT; 001-005, the MOTD.
# NickServ: IDENTIFY [account] password (900 and a notice), REGISTER password email (with
# --verify-code, a code "mailed": VERIFY REGISTER nick code), GHOST nick password.
# A bot, alice, in #bmds: a line every --chatter seconds, some of them mentioning whoever is
# there; bob joins and parts now and then.
# TestServ, for the board's tests: "KILL" closes your connection, "SILENT n" stops answering
# your PINGs for n seconds, "FLOOD n" sends you n lines in #bmds at once, "QUERY" has alice send you
# a private message, "KICKME" has alice kick you from #bmds, "INVITE" has alice invite you to #fun,
# "BOB" has bob quit or come back (MONITOR's and ISON's tests), "LISTWAIT" refuses your next LIST
# (263). alice answers CTCP VERSION, PING and TIME.
#
# Python's standard library and the openssl command only.

